demy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-353227" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353227" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\cot(\\theta)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-348096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polar curve $r=18,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-348096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-348096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-348096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-348096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\cot(\\theta)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-142382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=12$ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-142382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-142382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-142382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-142382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-142382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=30,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-353232" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353232" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/89ca909a3e3628e83e6aa4b2a20049ca33bd0436e130017caa9266fbea259022.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-353244" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353244" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sin(\\theta)+\\theta\\cos(\\theta)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-353249" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353249" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)+\\cos(\\theta)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-17115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/dfc16cf0da1332a921899dc1d3ebcdf75a59de3d1323c83f81a61c665b135ea8.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi },$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-17115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-17115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-17115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-17115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-17115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta },$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nOur strategy will be to compute ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =0,$ and then compute their ratio.\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r={e}^{\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ), \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\theta =0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\theta =0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(0)}{{x}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-353412" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-353412" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-141050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/ed90c6464c78010f38c764a677bddd3c7da22920fe4db62769e02d05934c9d24.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r={e}^{-3\\theta },$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-141050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r={e}^{-3\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-141050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba434e8e5648ca4419bdcce48409f4f84237c3b607c3c3e3061a0e349de04df7"} {"question" "q-141050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-141050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )"] ["math" "{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )"] ["math" "{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "q-141050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2f30200c03ff2c04bc89b5ec3e15ef1342fe19d42294c553761a92bdbd3f00d"} {"question" "e-9948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/a0eb20697f72b1ec5f06cc1ebfa4ba364c1023c915950b64cfab2367964db928.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta) .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-9948.json" "file_sha256" "10f430e596f475a663acdeb1363b2f0b25d1be6a8d0c07145a1f0943ff29c14b"} {"question" "e-9948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-9948.json" "file_sha256" "10f430e596f475a663acdeb1363b2f0b25d1be6a8d0c07145a1f0943ff29c14b"} {"question" "e-9949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta  - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-9949.json" "file_sha256" "886547a3ff703c974d2c4f931273d3f44ece29321b11fd0c5d4db9e0983f245c"} {"question" "e-9949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-9949.json" "file_sha256" "886547a3ff703c974d2c4f931273d3f44ece29321b11fd0c5d4db9e0983f245c"} {"question" "e-10034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c86e5a3dd066efc462635310ff80973f468d04bad9206f410a74941ccf39f2f3.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$  (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-10034.json" "file_sha256" "5d3aaa6d2a95632b58a5720723c7c81ca4ebaddfd51e6612d3e0814055304e90"} {"question" "e-10034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-10034.json" "file_sha256" "5d3aaa6d2a95632b58a5720723c7c81ca4ebaddfd51e6612d3e0814055304e90"} {"question" "e-628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/19b420204caf7f2706b4d355b5538dc347b5377d2b9f99297dbf6accc3220f59.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta,$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-628.json" "file_sha256" "c72200e05d959bd7202c36196bd6a23b820c6e5c3bd1383476ebd1d13b564f2d"} {"question" "e-628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/example-628.json" "file_sha256" "c72200e05d959bd7202c36196bd6a23b820c6e5c3bd1383476ebd1d13b564f2d"}] "usage" {"e-628" [] "e-9948" [] "q-17115" [] "e-9949" [] "e-10034" []} "basis" 1900 "retractions" [[17592186092542 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/q-17115.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the polar curve $r = \\frac{e^{θ}}{π}$, shown above."] [17592186092104 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n$y'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/e2076809a74372e836b4592936b7585b.png)"] [17592186092104 :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ))/(2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ}{\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092105 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, given that\n$y'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad x'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta$."] [17592186092105 :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) - \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= -\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/4} &= (y^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(x^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/4} &= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/(2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2} + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 2}{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186091871 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$ (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/b6c3d21c49c399c4407247101d36d938.png)"] [17592186091871 :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2$, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2\\cos θ, y = 2\\sin θ\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -2\\sin θ, y^{′}(θ) = 2\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2\\cos θ}{-2\\sin θ}; =-\\cot θ\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/3} &= -\\cot (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092035 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/11712254a9347c799840b9c88b9a8ee1.png)"] [17592186092035 :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$:\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (θ\\cos θ)^{′}; = (θ)^{′} \\cdot \\cos θ + θ \\cdot (\\cos θ)^{′}; = 1 \\cdot \\cos θ + θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = \\cos θ - θ\\sin θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (θ\\sin θ)^{′}; = (θ)^{′} \\cdot \\sin θ + θ \\cdot (\\sin θ)^{′}; = 1 \\cdot \\sin θ + θ \\cdot \\cos θ; = \\sin θ + θ\\cos θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sin θ + θ\\cos θ}{\\cos θ - θ\\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186089689 17592186089690 17592186089691 17592186089692 17592186089693] "immutable_fields" [[17592186089688 :answer-field/correct] [17592186089688 :answer-field/id] [17592186089688 :answer-field/key] [17592186089688 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
